{"id":"chatcmpl-dffeee79-a747-42f9-93a6-646dec15fbb7","object":"chat.completion","created":1795245875,"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"","content":"```json\n{\n  \"highlights\": \"This task demonstrates a verification-first approach for spatially ambiguous medical image interpretation, where the key challenge is resolving laterality on a projection that collapses depth. The successful strategy combines expert tool consultation with explicit risk assessment and iterative anatomical reasoning before committing to an answer. Patterns emphasize using anatomical landmarks as ground truth and treating ambiguous projections as requiring extra confirmation.\",\n  \"patterns\": [\n    \"When a projection collapses spatial dimensions (e.g., lateral view obscuring left/right), use anatomical landmarks as reference anchors rather than raw visual position.\",\n    \"Before finalizing an answer on ambiguous evidence, explicitly articulate the specific risk (e.g., 'laterality may be misjudged due to projection') to trigger targeted verification.\",\n    \"Iterate expert queries with progressively refined anatomical framing (landmark-relative location, then trajectory/insertion-path inference) when a single query yields uncertain laterality.\",\n    \"Cross-check the proposed answer against a second independent reasoning pass (expert re-query or verifier) before release, especially when the answer hinges on a single ambiguous feature.\"\n  ],\n  \"playbooks\": {\n    \"Medical imaging interpretation\": \"Anchor laterality and location judgments to fixed anatomical landmarks (spine, sternum, heart shadow) rather than image-space position; use trajectory/insertion-path reasoning to infer side when direct visualization is ambiguous.\",\n    \"Ambiguous-evidence decision tasks\": \"Name the specific ambiguity and its failure mode before answering, then run a targeted confirmation query aimed exactly at that ambiguity.\",\n    \"Tool-assisted QA\": \"Chain expert calls: first detect presence, then localize relative to landmarks, then infer orientation/trajectory; escalate query specificity only as needed.\"\n  },\n  \"checklists\": [\n    \"Confirm the finding's laterality/location using at least one anatomical landmark reference, not just visual position.\",\n    \"State the specific risk of misjudgment and verify the answer addresses it before finalizing.\"\n  ]\n}\n```"},"finish_reason":"stop","logprobs":null}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":582,"total_tokens":988,"completion_tokens":406,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":582},"completion_tokens_details":{"text_tokens":406}}}